{"id": "Db5pSuxtXuE", "title": "Video by krishnachaytanyaa", "description": "SQL injection, explained in 9 seconds.\n\nThe database was never fooled. Your code was.\n\nYour SQL query and whatever the user typed get glued into ONE string before anything is sent — so by the time it arrives, there is no seam left to find. A SQL parser walks that string in two modes: command, and quoted text. Their own quote flips it back to command early, so OR 1=1 RUNS as code instead of being searched for, and -- comments out the quote that was supposed to close it. One row asked for. Forty rows returned.\n\nThat is also why escaping keeps failing. It works everywhere you remember to do it, and one missed call site is the whole breach.\n\nThe fix is not a better filter. A parameterised query — a prepared statement — sends the structure and the value on two separate channels: the query is fixed FIRST, then the value is bound into a slot where it can never become structure. Same malicious string, zero rows, failed login.\n\nSame in Postgres, MySQL and every driver worth using. OWASP has kept this at the top of the list for two decades because the concatenation is still one line away in every codebase.\n\n#sqlinjection #systemdesign #backend #cybersecurity #softwareengineering", "timestamp": 1786453224, "channel": "krishnachaytanyaa", "uploader": "Krishna Chaitanya | Solutions Architect", "uploader_id": "27131031756", "like_count": 1412, "comment_count": 9, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "duration": 9.045000076293945, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1737448400927068a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 31.517, "asr": 48000, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr20-2.fna.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m78/AQMe6haekbs0bAeObT6bJanxDrAROjBk7TeD5dhVtxbz79Ofqa4TeONBZmp9RMUZcGUkAORea7Vi27resZ2t3ANNpdA1diVgj-swYVo.mp4?_nc_cat=111&_nc_oc=AdpSU4W3ufn9N2hKJdWPm_q_l8Tw_0HhxTS0eGz3WN8ahfU_bIQ5kvjppzauSiHzcN4K